@node Agent Types
@section Agent Types
@cindex agent types
@cindex prompts

An @emph{agent type} fixes the role and the prompt protocol.  The built-in
types are Task, Review, Plan, QA, and Custom.  Each type has two prompts:

@itemize
@item
a role-only @emph{system} prompt
(@code{beads-agent-type-system-prompt}), and
@item
an issue-envelope @emph{user} prompt
(@code{beads-agent-type-build-user-prompt}).
@end itemize

@deftypefn {Emacs Lisp Function} {} beads-agent-type-system-prompt (type)
Return the role-only system prompt for an agent TYPE.
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn {Emacs Lisp Function} {} beads-agent-type-build-user-prompt (type issue)
Return the issue-envelope user prompt for TYPE and ISSUE.
@end deftypefn

Settings are kept in @code{beads-agent-type.el}.  Custom types register in
the same way and appear in the menu.

@node Agent Backends
@section Agent Backends
@cindex agent backends
@cindex backend registry

A @emph{backend} knows how to start one program in one terminal.  Backends
register in a registry (@code{beads-agent-backend.el}); the shipped set
includes:

@itemize
@item
@code{claude-code}, @code{claude-code-ide}, @code{claudemacs},
@code{eca}, and @code{agent-shell};
@item
@code{mock}, for tests; and
@item
@code{terminal}, an opt-in collision-free backend that spawns a CLI
directly into a terminal.
@end itemize

@deftypefn {Emacs Lisp Function} {} beads-agent-backend-start (backend issue system-prompt user-prompt)
Start BACKEND for ISSUE with the given SYSTEM-PROMPT and USER-PROMPT.
@end deftypefn

Backends are selected by name.
@vindex beads-agent-curated-backends
@code{beads-agent-curated-backends} limits the menu to a preferred subset
and leaves the rest selectable but out of the way.

@node Sessions and Worktrees
@section Sessions and Worktrees
@cindex worktree isolation

A session is registered in the in-house registry as soon as it starts, so
it can be revisited after a restart.  When git worktree isolation is
enabled, the agent runs in its own worktree with its own branch, so
parallel agents do not fight over the working tree.  The worktree helpers
live in @code{beads-git.el} and are shared with the terminal and swarm
modules.

@node Sling and Swarm
@section Sling and Swarm
@anchor{sling}
@cindex sling
@cindex swarm

@emph{Sling} dispatches a bead to an agent or another target.
@emph{Swarm} runs many agents over the children of an epic in waves.

@table @kbd
@kindex S
@item S
From the dispatch menu, open the swarm fleet list
(@code{beads-swarm-list-view}).
@kindex w
@item w
From the maintenance menu, open the swarm sub-dispatch.
@end table

@anchor{fig-swarm-dark}
@shot{swarm-dark, The swarm fleet list showing wave progress.}

Sling is preview-first.  Pressing @kbd{s} in the sling menu opens the
full preview instead of dispatching immediately; the preview shows the
header sentence, the validation checks, the recipe's steps and the
routing plan, so you can adjust the work, formula, target and variables
before committing.  From the preview, @kbd{s} or @kbd{C-c C-c} launches
exactly what was previewed, and @kbd{q} aborts without dispatching.  The
preview never gates launch: any warnings it shows are advisory and the
same checks feed the live menu footer.

The How stage lists every variable the chosen formula accepts, including
variables inherited through @code{extends}: a workflow such as
@code{build-basic} shows the @code{artifact_root} and other variables
declared on its @code{build-base} parent.  Sling and swarm share the
terminal subsystem with the other agent flows.
